From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 13:04:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 23/45] fix(deacon): confirm auto-push outages against the
 remote ref, not push-state (gcp-llja)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The push-state divergence detector (gcp-qhx1, merged 5e820ed) treated a frozen
`push-state.json.last_commit` as proof that auto-push was not landing. That
inference assumed bd records a success whenever a push actually lands, and bd
does not: it wraps each auto-push in `dolt.auto-push-timeout` (default 30s) and
takes the FAILURE path when the timer fires — recording last_push but not
last_commit — even though the push completes server-side moments later.

So on any store whose pushes routinely outrun that timeout, the watermark froze
permanently while the remote kept advancing, and the detector read that as a
sustained outage and escalated CRITICAL to the mayor forever. Measured on winnow
2026-08-14 00:16-00:56Z: last_commit frozen at fqvpipur... for 40 minutes while
remotes/origin/main moved 4o9gc4ck... -> 755lvh7f... in lockstep with last_push,
with no fetch by the observer. A routine winnow push of a ~7min backlog measured
41s against the 30s default, so essentially every cycle recorded failure.

The watermark is now only the cheap first filter — it is free and it correctly
flags every suspicious store — and the verdict is drawn from the remote's actual
position, read out of that scope's own dolt_remote_branches. STALE now means the
REMOTE is >= threshold behind local head. A frozen watermark over a current
remote gets a new verdict, FROZEN: not an outage, but a real condition worth
surfacing, because it means bd's change-detection is broken on that store and a
genuine outage there would be indistinguishable from the state it is already in.
mol-deacon-patrol nudges FROZEN rather than paging, and clears the STALE
escalation marker on it (pushes are landing again) without clearing its own.

Deliberate: the check does NOT fetch. `dolt push` updates the tracking ref
itself, so the ref advances unaided on a store that auto-pushes — the winnow
trace shows exactly that — and a ref that IS stale reads as "remote behind",
which biases toward STALE. Declining to fetch can therefore only make this alert
more, never less, so the real-outage path is not weakened. The reasoning is
written into the script's header rather than left implicit. Where the remote's
position cannot be established at all (no tracking ref, remote head absent from
the local log, unreadable) the verdict is UNKNOWN, never STALE: an unproven
outage must not page.

Validation:
- The detector suite goes red->green: the four new tests fail 12 assertions
  against the pre-fix script and pass against this one.
- Test 2b replays the measured winnow 00:16-00:56 trace as a fixture (acceptance
  1); test 2 keeps the 18h-outage and exact 899/900 boundary cases green
  (acceptance 2); test 2c pins two scopes with byte-identical watermarks to
  FROZEN vs STALE purely on the remote's position (acceptance 3).
- Reproduced end-to-end against a live Dolt store with a doctored watermark: the
  pre-fix script reports STALE 85936s "auto-push not landing", this one reports
  FROZEN with remotes/origin/main 2 commits / 97s behind.
- All nine gastown/tests suites pass; shellcheck, gofumpt, go vet, go build,
  go test -race, golangci-lint and validate_registry all clean.

Also gates `pass` in the detector suite on the failure count, so a test that
fails four assertions can no longer print an "ok:" line under them — that
misleading green showed up while verifying the red state here.

Pre-existing and untouched: tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py fails on
origin/integration for unrelated files (stub case labels and comments in
gastown/tests/test_merge_approval_gate.sh and test_refinery_find_work.sh).
Filed as gcp-mvsm, discovered-from this bead, rather than fixed inline.
